{
  "description": "TAI Julian Dates converted to TT, TDB, TCG, TCB and GPS, cross-checked against astropy. Each Julian Date is a two-part value [jd1, jd2] so the comparison stays below a nanosecond, where a single float would lose tens of microseconds. The cases are anchor dates, among them the 1977-01-01 TAI origin TCG and TCB are defined from and the days either side of it, a deterministic sweep across the domain crossed with a palette of day fractions, and a sub-second stress at two dates.",
  "source": {"astropy": "7.2.0", "pyerfa": "2.0.1.5", "method": "astropy.time.Time(jd1, jd2, format='jd', scale='tai'); TT, TDB, TCG and TCB read as .tt/.tdb/.tcg/.tcb jd1/jd2. No observer location is set, so TDB is geocentric, matching Horologium::Data::BarycentricModel. GPS has no scale of its own in astropy, since it is a defining 19 SI second offset from TAI, so it is taken as Time - TimeDelta(19, format='sec'), which keeps astropy's two-part arithmetic rather than restating the offset."},
